statement:
For positive real numbers $a, b, c$, which of the following statements necessarily implies $a = b = c$:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(I)] $a(b^3 + c^3) = b(c^3 + a^3) = c(a^3 + b^3)$,
\item[(II)] $a(a^3 + b^3) = b(b^3 + c^3) = c(c^3 + a^3)$?
\end{itemize}
Justify your answer.

Let $\mathbb{N}$ denote the set of natural numbers. Define a function $T : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ by $T(2k) = k$ and $T(2k+1) = 2k+2$. We write $T^2(n) = T(T(n))$ and in general $T^k(n) = T^{k-1}(T(n))$ for any $k > 1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] Show that for each $n \in \mathbb{N}$, there exists $k$ such that $T^k(n) = 1$.
\item[(ii)] For $k \in \mathbb{N}$, let $c_k$ denote the number of elements in the set $\{n : T^k(n) = 1\}$. Prove that $c_{k+2} = c_{k+1} + c_k$ for $k \geq 1$.
\end{enumerate}
